"Insulated walls are perfect mirrors: every bounce is elastic, so the gas keeps its energy and T only changes when the piston moves. Pick All to set every wall at once, or one wall to set it alone. Walls held at a temperature are in contact with a large body at that temperature. A molecule that hits one trades energy with it, on the fraction of hits set by Hits that trade energy (the accommodation coefficient α, from {#}% to {#}%), and leaves as if it came from a gas at the wall's temperature; otherwise it bounces like a mirror. So a hotter gas gives heat to the walls and a colder one takes heat from them until T reaches the wall temperature, faster when more hits exchange energy. With walls at different temperatures the gas never settles to one T: heat flows steadily through it from the warm walls to the cold ones, and the half of the box near the warm wall stays warmer. Q adds up the heat the walls give the gas since the start (negative when the gas gives heat away); {#} zJ is {#}⁻²¹ J. The walls glow blue when they are colder than the gas and orange when warmer. Real surfaces exchange anywhere from a small fraction to nearly all of the energy, depending on the gas and how clean and rough the surface is.",
